<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    Off-state default for ufmStatusSuccessIcon / WarningIcon / ErrorIcon
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    A transparent no-op. When Colorblind Mode is Off, all three status-glyph
    attributes resolve here, so a caller that applies a glyph unconditionally
    draws nothing rather than a wrong or duplicated icon.

    WHY A TRANSPARENT DEFAULT RATHER THAN A REAL GLYPH

    The ~40 status call sites are a mix of two kinds:

      • sites that already render a status icon of their own, and
      • sites that convey status by COLOUR alone.

    A single default cannot be both "keep your existing icon" (which would need
    to point at that site's icon) and "you have no icon to add". Pointing the
    default at a real glyph would change every colour-only site the moment the
    refactor lands, breaking FR-19's requirement that Off restores the app's
    appearance exactly — and it would silently replace the existing icon at
    sites that already have one.

    So the contract is the inverse: callers apply a glyph ONLY while
    ColorblindPalette is enabled, restoring the site's own drawable otherwise.
    See ColorblindPalette.statusIcon and task T041.
-->
<shape xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="rectangle">
    <solid android:color="@android:color/transparent" />
</shape>
